CAT = "rbtData"; SEG = "SDAT"
Q = [
# A.1 continuous measurement
("A client's goal is to start a worksheet sooner after the teacher says \"Begin.\" To measure latency, the technician should start the timer when:",
 "The teacher finishes \"Begin\"", ["The client picks up a pencil", "The worksheet is set down", "The first problem is done"],
 "Latency runs from the end of the instruction to the start of the response. Starting when the client picks up the pencil would time nothing, since that is the response itself."),
("A client hums loudly in episodes lasting from a few seconds to several minutes. The supervisor wants to know how much of each session the humming takes up. The technician should record:",
 "Total duration of humming", ["Frequency of humming", "Latency to each episode", "Interresponse time per hum"],
 "Total duration shows how much of the session the behavior fills. A frequency count would treat a 3-second hum and a 5-minute hum the same."),
("The supervisor wants to know whether a client is eating more slowly at lunch. The technician times the gap from the end of one bite to the start of the next. This records:",
 "Interresponse time", ["Response latency", "Duration of each bite", "Rate of bites per minute"],
 "Interresponse time (IRT) is the time between two successive responses. Latency would be timed from an instruction or cue to the response, not from one bite to the next."),
("A plan defines a \"flop\" as sitting on the floor, with a new flop counted only after 10 seconds of standing. A client sits, stands for 4 seconds, and sits again. The technician should record:",
 "Count it as one flop", ["Count it as two flops", "Count it as three flops", "Count none until it ends"],
 "The client stood for less than the 10 seconds the definition requires, so the second sit is part of the same flop. Recording two flops ignores the onset rule in the definition."),
("A client has tantrums about twice a day, but some last 40 minutes. The team wants to know whether the tantrums are getting shorter. Which measure fits best?",
 "Duration per episode", ["Frequency per day", "Partial interval recording", "Latency to each tantrum"],
 "Duration per episode directly shows whether each tantrum is getting shorter. Frequency would stay at about two a day even if tantrums dropped from 40 minutes to 5."),
# A.2 discontinuous measurement
("Using 15-second partial interval recording, the technician sees a client bite a nail for 2 seconds at the start of an interval and then stop. The technician should:",
 "Score the interval", ["Leave it unscored", "Score half the interval", "Score the next one too"],
 "In partial interval recording any occurrence during the interval scores it, however brief. Leaving it unscored would apply the whole interval rule by mistake."),
("Using 10-second whole interval recording for on-task behavior, a client works for 9 seconds, then looks away for the last second. The interval should be:",
 "Not scored for on-task", ["Scored for on-task", "Scored as partly on-task", "Recorded as missing data"],
 "Whole interval recording scores an interval only if the behavior lasts the entire interval. Nine of ten seconds is not the whole interval, so it is not scored."),
("Using 1-minute momentary time sampling, a client is off task for most of an interval but is on task at the moment the minute ends. The technician should record:",
 "On task for that interval", ["Off task for that interval", "Nothing for that interval", "On task for the next one"],
 "Momentary time sampling records only whether the behavior is occurring at the moment the interval ends. What happened earlier in the minute is not counted."),
("A supervisor picks a discontinuous measure for off-task behavior that the plan aims to reduce, wanting any occurrence in an interval to count. The technician will most likely use:",
 "Partial interval recording", ["Whole interval recording", "Momentary time sampling", "Permanent product recording"],
 "Partial interval recording scores any occurrence and tends to overestimate, which suits behaviors to reduce. Whole interval recording suits behaviors to increase."),
("Halfway through a phase, a technician switches from whole interval to partial interval recording for the same on-task goal without telling anyone. The graph will most likely show:",
 "A jump that is not real", ["A true gain in on-task", "A drop that is not real", "No change in the data"],
 "Partial interval recording scores more intervals than whole interval for the same behavior, so on-task will look higher. The change comes from the measure, not the client."),
("During a 20-minute session, a technician checks for on-task behavior every 2 minutes using momentary time sampling. The client is on task at 6 of the checks. What should be recorded?",
 "60% of checks on task", ["30% of checks on task", "6% of checks on task", "40% of checks on task"],
 "There are 20 / 2 = 10 checks, and 6 / 10 x 100 = 60%. Dividing by the 20 minutes instead of the 10 checks gives 30%."),
# A.3 permanent product
("A client's goal is to make the bed each morning before the technician arrives at 9 a.m. The technician can best measure this by:",
 "Checking the bed on arrival", ["Asking the client at 9 a.m.", "Timing the task the next day", "Asking a parent each week"],
 "Permanent product recording measures the lasting result of the behavior, a made bed, after it happens. Asking the client or parent is indirect report, not a direct measure."),
("A technician scores a client's math worksheet by permanent product but saw a sibling write several of the answers. The best step is to:",
 "Note it for the supervisor", ["Score the sheet as written", "Discard it without a note", "Have the client redo the sheet"],
 "Permanent product data are valid only if the client made the product. Scoring it as written records a sibling's work, and discarding it silently hides the problem."),
# A.4 enter data and update graphs
("A client's plan changes to a new teaching procedure after sessions with 6, 5 and 4 correct. Before plotting the first data point under the new procedure, the technician should:",
 "Add a phase change line", ["Start a new y-axis scale", "Connect it to the last point", "Wait for three new points"],
 "A phase change line marks where the condition changed, and data paths are not connected across it. Connecting the new point to the old phase blurs the effect of the change."),
("Data are graphed by calendar day, and the client was absent Tuesday. For Tuesday, the technician should:",
 "Leave the day with no point", ["Enter a zero for that day", "Copy Monday's value", "Average Monday and Wednesday"],
 "No session means no data, so no point is plotted. A zero would wrongly show that the behavior was measured and did not occur."),
("A technician notices that last week's paper data sheet shows 7 requests, but she typed 17 into the graph. She should:",
 "Fix it and tell the supervisor", ["Leave it as an outlier", "Delete that data point", "Fix the graph with no note made"],
 "Data must match the record, and the supervisor needs to know a graph was wrong because it may have shaped decisions. Fixing it silently hides the error."),
("Sessions vary in length from 30 to 90 minutes. To graph a client's requests so sessions can be compared, the technician should plot:",
 "Requests per minute", ["Total count per session", "Percent of trials correct", "Longest gap between requests"],
 "Rate (count divided by time) makes sessions of different lengths comparable. A raw count would rise in longer sessions even if the client requested no more often."),
# A.5 observable and measurable definitions
("A team is writing a definition of \"elopement\" for a client who leaves group areas. Which definition would let two observers agree most often?",
 "Exits the room unaccompanied", ["Tries to escape from adults", "Runs off when frustrated by work", "Wanders away when bored"],
 "Exiting the room without an adult can be seen and counted. The others depend on guesses about the client's intent or feelings, so observers would disagree."),
("After a session, a technician writes a note about an incident at the play table. Which entry describes the client's behavior objectively?",
 "Threw two blocks at the wall", ["Was angry about the blocks", "Got upset and acted out loudly", "Wanted attention from staff"],
 "An objective entry states what was seen, here what was thrown, how many and where. The other notes infer an emotion or a motive the technician cannot observe."),
("A plan defines aggression as \"being mean to peers.\" The technician finds this hard to score. The best step is to:",
 "Ask the supervisor to clarify", ["Write her own clearer definition", "Count only clear hitting", "Keep scoring as written"],
 "Unclear definitions produce unreliable data, and the supervisor owns the plan. Writing her own definition or counting only hitting would change the plan without direction."),
("While recording ABC data during circle time, a technician writes down what happened just before an outburst. Which antecedent entry is observable?",
 "Teacher said \"Clean up now\"", ["Teacher pushed him too hard", "Client was tired from school", "Task was too boring for him"],
 "The teacher's words can be heard and recorded. The other entries are judgments about effort, tiredness or boredom that an observer cannot see."),
("A definition of on-task behavior lists \"staring out the window\" as a non-example. What is the purpose of the non-example?",
 "To mark what does not count", ["To list behaviors to punish", "To make the count go down", "To replace the measure used"],
 "Non-examples show the edges of a definition so observers agree on borderline cases. They do not name behaviors for punishment."),
# A.6 calculate and summarize data
("A client makes 18 vocal requests during a 45-minute session. What is the rate per minute?",
 "0.4 requests per minute", ["2.5 requests per minute", "1.8 requests per minute", "0.04 requests per minute"],
 "Rate is count divided by time: 18 / 45 = 0.4 requests per minute. Dividing time by count gives 2.5, which is minutes per request."),
("On Monday a client made 10 requests in 20 minutes. On Tuesday the client made 15 requests in 60 minutes. Compared with Monday, Tuesday's rate:",
 "Was half of Monday's rate", ["Was 1.5 times Monday's rate", "Was equal to Monday's rate", "Was 3 times Monday's rate"],
 "Monday is 10 / 20 = 0.5 per minute and Tuesday is 15 / 60 = 0.25 per minute, so Tuesday's rate is half. Comparing raw counts (15 against 10) gives 1.5 times, which ignores time."),
("A client has three tantrums lasting 30 seconds, 2 minutes and 4 minutes 30 seconds. What is the mean duration?",
 "2 minutes 20 seconds", ["Exactly 2 minutes", "Exactly 7 minutes", "2 minutes 30 seconds"],
 "Total duration is 0.5 + 2 + 4.5 = 7 minutes, and 7 / 3 is about 2.33 minutes, or 2 minutes 20 seconds. Two minutes is the middle value (median), not the mean."),
("A technician uses 10-second partial interval recording for 5 minutes and scores 12 intervals. What percentage of intervals had the behavior?",
 "40% of intervals", ["12% of intervals", "60% of intervals", "30% of intervals"],
 "Five minutes is 300 seconds, so there are 300 / 10 = 30 intervals, and 12 / 30 x 100 = 40%. Using 12 as a percentage skips the division."),
("Of 20 trials, a client answers 14 correctly without help, 3 correctly after a prompt, and 3 incorrectly. The plan counts only independent correct responses. What is recorded?",
 "70% of trials correct", ["85% of trials correct", "15% of trials correct", "82% of trials correct"],
 "Only independent correct responses count: 14 / 20 x 100 = 70%. Adding the 3 prompted responses gives 17 / 20 = 85%, which the plan does not allow."),
("Two technicians count a client's hand raises in the same session. One records 16 and the other 20. What is the total count IOA?",
 "80% total count IOA", ["125% total count IOA", "20% total count IOA", "96% total count IOA"],
 "Total count IOA is the smaller count divided by the larger: 16 / 20 x 100 = 80%. Dividing the larger by the smaller gives 125%, which is impossible for agreement."),
("A primary observer records 27 instances of a behavior and an IOA observer records 30 in the same session. What is the total count IOA?",
 "90% total count IOA", ["111% total count IOA", "10% total count IOA", "97% total count IOA"],
 "Smaller divided by larger: 27 / 30 x 100 = 90%. Dividing 30 by 27 gives about 111%, and 10% is only the difference between the counts as a share of 30."),
# A.7 trends in graphed data
("Before teaching starts, a client's baseline shows correct responses of 2, 4, 6 and 8 across sessions. Why might the supervisor delay starting the intervention?",
 "The skill is already rising", ["The data are too variable", "The level is too low to teach", "The trend is going down"],
 "A rising baseline means the skill is improving without the intervention, so later gains could not be credited to teaching. The data are steady, not variable."),
("During intervention, a client's aggression per session is 9, 2, 10, 1, 8 and 3. Which description fits best?",
 "High variability, no trend", ["A clear decreasing trend", "Stable at a low level overall", "A steady increasing trend"],
 "The values swing between high and low with no steady direction, which is high variability. The low points alone could be mistaken for a decreasing trend."),
("After the intervention starts, a client's tantrums drop right away from about 12 per day to about 3 per day and stay there. This is best described as:",
 "An immediate drop in level", ["A gradually decreasing trend", "An increase in variability", "A delayed change in level"],
 "A sudden shift from one level to another at the phase change is a change in level. A trend would show a gradual slope over sessions."),
# A.8 risks of unreliable data and poor fidelity
("Using partial interval recording, a technician sometimes looks away to set up materials and scores those intervals as no behavior. The data will most likely:",
 "Underestimate the behavior", ["Overestimate the behavior", "Stay accurate on average", "Show more variability only"],
 "Intervals scored as no behavior when nobody was watching miss occurrences, so the behavior looks lower than it is. Partial interval recording overestimates only when it is run correctly."),
("Two technicians run the same token plan, but one gives tokens for prompted responses and the other does not. Their data agree closely. The risk is that the data:",
 "Reflect two different plans", ["Are valid since IOA is high", "Show only the token count", "Favor the stricter technician"],
 "High agreement shows observers recorded the same thing, not that the plan was run the same way. With poor procedural fidelity, the graph mixes two procedures and cannot show which works."),
("Over several months, a technician starts counting soft taps as \"hitting,\" though the definition excludes them. This change in scoring is called:",
 "Observer drift", ["Observer reactivity", "Response generalization", "Interobserver agreement"],
 "Observer drift is a gradual, unplanned change in how an observer applies a definition. Reactivity is a change in the client's behavior because the client knows it is being observed."),
("A client's problem behavior drops sharply only on days when the supervisor comes to observe. This threat to the data is called:",
 "Reactivity to observation", ["Drift in observer scoring", "A spontaneous recovery", "An extinction burst pattern"],
 "Reactivity means behavior changes because it is being observed, so those sessions do not show typical behavior. Observer drift is a change in the observer, not the client."),
]
